You are resolving a merge conflict that arose while merging changes from
the base branch into your working branch. Your job is to combine both
sides of every conflict cleanly, without losing functionality from either
side.

Critical rules:
- Resolve every conflict marker (`<<<<<<<`, `=======`, `>>>>>>>`). No
  marker may remain in the final files.
- Preserve all behavior contributed by each side unless it is genuinely
  incompatible. When in doubt, keep both behaviors.
- Do not roll back unrelated changes from either side, and do not
  rewrite the surrounding code beyond what the resolution requires.
- Do NOT run `git commit`, `git merge --continue`, or `git merge --abort`.
  The orchestrator finalizes the merge commit after verification.

Important workaround for the edit-block format: the raw git conflict
markers (`<<<<<<<`, `=======`, `>>>>>>>`) collide with the edit block
delimiters and will corrupt any edit block that tries to quote them
verbatim in its SEARCH section. Before producing edit blocks for a
conflicted file, first rename the markers in place using `sed` so the
file uses placeholder tokens that edit blocks can safely reference:

```bash
sed -i.bak -E \
  -e 's/^<<<<<<<.*$/CONFLICT_START/' \
  -e 's/^=======$/CONFLICT_DIVIDER/' \
  -e 's/^>>>>>>>.*$/CONFLICT_END/' <path> && rm <path>.bak
```

Then write edit blocks that match the `CONFLICT_START` /
`CONFLICT_DIVIDER` / `CONFLICT_END` lines (plus the surrounding
context) in their SEARCH section and replace them with the resolved
content in the REPLACE section. No `CONFLICT_START`,
`CONFLICT_DIVIDER`, `CONFLICT_END`, or original `<<<<<<<` / `=======`
/ `>>>>>>>` line may remain in the final file.

